# /etc/udev/rules.d/90-jack-bridge-usb-audio.rules
#
# Keep jackd on the right card when an audio interface is plugged in or pulled
# out. This matters because jack-bridge runs jackd directly ON a USB interface
# rather than bridging it through alsa_out: unplugging the interface takes the
# server down with it, and replugging it does not bring the server back, because
# jackd-rt only chooses its device at start.
#
# KERNEL=="card[0-9]*" deliberately matches only the card node. The sound
# subsystem also emits an event per pcm and control node (pcmC0D0p, controlC0,
# ...), and matching those would fire the handler a dozen times for one cable.
#
# The handler detaches itself and returns immediately, because RUN+= is meant for
# very short tasks and a jackd restart is not one. See the long comment in
# jack-usb-hotplug for why a detached grandchild survives under eudev, checked
# against its source rather than taken from udev(7), whose warning describes
# systemd-udevd's cgroup kill.
#
# It then settles, takes a lock, and does nothing unless jackd is actually on the
# wrong card -- so the remaining duplicate events cost nothing.
#
# This rule needs udev only as a notification. Nothing else in jack-bridge
# depends on it: if the rule never fires, the stack behaves exactly as it did
# before hotplug recovery existed.

ACTION=="add",    SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="card[0-9]*", RUN+="/usr/local/lib/jack-bridge/jack-usb-hotplug add"
ACTION=="remove", SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="card[0-9]*", RUN+="/usr/local/lib/jack-bridge/jack-usb-hotplug remove"
